//@ expect: accept
//@ run: 15
//@ reason: `total` is assigned from `base`, a field of the same class that this constructor assigns AFTER it is
//@ reason: declared but that is not itself hoisted (its assignment comes second). An initializer expression may only
//@ reason: read parameters and fields hoisted earlier; reading any other own field from the initializer list would
//@ reason: depend on C++'s declaration-order construction instead of on the order the statements were written. The
//@ reason: statement stays in the body, so `total` sees `base` after `base = v`.
//@ reason: (A guard for the rule: the compiler before the fix also happened to print this, because the field read was in order.)

class Sum {
public:
    int base;
    int total;
    Sum(int v) {
        base = v;
        total = base + 8;
    }
};

int main() {
    Sum s = new Sum(7);
    std::cout << s.total << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
